|passage=So the great wasteful summer days went by, the glory of the passionate nights of July, the crisper '''blonde''' luxuriance of August.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Arturo Pérez-Reverte|chapter_tlr=w:Margaret Sayers Peden|chapter=The Lost Ship|title=The Nautical Chart|location=San Diego, Calif.|publisher=[[w:Harcourt (publisher)|Harcourt, Inc.]]|year=2001|page=78|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nauticalchart00pr/page/78/mode/1up|isbn=0-15-100534-6|passage=In a bar in Copenhagen, for example, filled with blond men and '''blonde''' women who in the end turned out to be more blond men, the Torpedoman got riled because when he copped a feel he found a handful of something he hadn’t expected.|origtext=es:Como en aquel bar de Copenhague lleno de hombres rubios y de mujeres '''[[rubio|rubias]]''' que al final resultaron ser también hombres rubios, donde el Torpedero Tucumán se había enfadado porque al meter mano se encontró quinientos buenos gramos de lo que no esperaba; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Meg Burden|chapter=9|title=Northlander|series=Tales of the Borderlands|seriesvolume=1|location=Weston, Conn.|publisher=Brown Barn Books|year=2007|page=67|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/northlander0000burd/page/67/mode/1up|isbn=978-09768126-8-5|passage=The tables are full of blond men and '''blonde''' women, the room full of the smells of food and the sound of harsh, clipped Northlander voices.}}
# {{lb|en|especially|of a|woman|offensive}} [[stupid|Stupid]], [[ignorant]], [[naive]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Nancy Bartholomew|title=Lethally Blonde|publisher=Silhouette|isbn=9781552543771|page=8
|passage=Emma&#39;s already huge green eyes widen and she gives me this look like, “Oh my God, sometimes you are just so '''blonde'''!”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Aviva Drescher|title=Leggy Blonde: A Memoir|publisher=Simon and Schuster|isbn=9781476722153
|passage=I was so '''blonde''' I honestly had no idea why he got so angry. He cooled on me for a week or so.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Bennett Fairorth|title=The Land Where My American Mother Died--Palermo, Sicily|page=201|publisher=iUniverse|isbn=9780595279388
|passage=“She was so '''blonde''', she studied for a blood test, when she went to the airport and saw a sign that said, &#39;Airport Left,&#39; she turned around and went home, when she heard that 90% of all crimes occur around the home, she moved.}}

